---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { release, url } from '../lib/publication';
const rain = release.claims.find(c => c.id === 'C-IMD-RAINFALL-DEP-india-20260923');
const access = release.claims.find(c => c.id === 'C-NSO-PREMISES-2018');
---
<Site title="Who bears the cost?" description="The documented access gap and the local records needed before assigning 2026 economic or household losses.">
  <PageHeader title="Who bears the cost?" eyebrow="Livelihoods and unequal exposure" description="A rainfall shortfall can pass through farms, services, work and household budgets—but each consequence needs its own evidence." />
  <section class="section"><div class="shell page-grid"><article>
    <p class="eyebrow">The question before the headline</p><h2>Exposure is not a measured loss.</h2><p>IMD's <a href={rain ? url(`/sources/${rain.evidenceIds[0]}/`) : url('/sources/')}>1 June–23 September 2026 rainfall report</a> describes weather at national and broad regional scales. It does not count damaged crops, lost wages, tanker spending, food-price changes or migration. We have no approved 2026 outcome series for those effects, and no defensible national loss total.</p>
    <div class="figure"><p class="eyebrow">Follow a possible effect; test each link</p><div class="table-wrap"><table class="data-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Pathway</th><th scope="col">What would make it observable?</th><th scope="col">What this release knows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Farms and food</td><td>Local rain timing, crop stage, irrigation, sowing, yield and market records at matched dates.</td><td>No approved crop or price outcome linked to this rainfall.</td></tr><tr><td>Work and business</td><td>Dated service disruptions, production records and affected workforce in a defined place.</td><td>No approved loss estimate.</td></tr><tr><td>Household costs</td><td>Service hours, purchased-water prices, collection time and income or tenure context.</td><td>Older access surveys; no September 2026 household cost estimate.</td></tr><tr><td>Health and quality</td><td>Local water tests and competent health authority records.</td><td>No current quality or illness inference from rain.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
    <h2>The unequal starting point is visible, but dated.</h2><p>In a <strong>2018 national household survey</strong>, 58.2% of rural households and 80.7% of urban households reported a drinking-water facility within their premises. That is one measure of access eight years before this rain snapshot; it is not a 2026 measure of service or an explanation of the difference. <a href={access ? url(`/sources/${access.evidenceIds[0]}/`) : url('/sources/')}>Read the NSO survey record ↗</a></p><p>Money can sometimes buy storage, treatment or a temporary supply, while pipes, aquifers, electricity, workers and markets remain shared systems. Whether those buffers were used, how much they cost and who lacked them is a place-specific reporting question. We will not invent two “typical” households to make it vivid.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>What would establish an impact?</strong><p>Pair a dated local outcome with the relevant water-system record, then test other explanations. Even a simultaneous price rise and rainfall deficit would not by itself establish causation.</p></div>
  </article><aside><p class="eyebrow">Continue</p><a href={url('/water-and-people/')}>Access, connections and functionality</a><a href={url('/journalists/')}>Records to request</a><a href={url('/states/')}>Start with a place</a><a href={url('/data-status/')}>Outcome coverage</a></aside></div></section>
</Site>
